ge tenant/resource authority. Use unknown for unavailable implementation evidence.\nSaved E06.3 = no. Next: E06.4 — If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n  How to verify: 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\nSaved E06.4 = no. Next: E06.5 — Under MCP 2026-07-28, relevant header/body mismatches are rejected before routing or policy relies on them, and private caches/state/task handles are isolated by verified authorization context, tenant, resource and policy version.\n  How to verify: 1) Identify supported protocol versions. 2) For 2026-07-28, trace version/method/name/parameter header validation before affected routing/policy decisions. 3) Trace cache keys/invalidation and every task/state-handle operation, including status/results and cancellation where present. 4) Reject cross-tenant/caller reads or stale grants after revocation; permit shared cache entries only when explicitly public. 5) Do not require removed protocol sessions for 2026-07-28; test legacy session binding only when that version is enabled.\nSaved E06.5 = unknown. Next: E06.6 — If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.\n  How to verify: 1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check RFC 9207 issuer-response comparison and rejection when an advertised required iss is missing; do not require an absent iss when the selected protocol permits it. Check it blocks unauthorized private, loopback, and metadata destinations, including on redirects and DNS